\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {II}{\ignorespaces Derived measures against a published asymptomatic reference ($n=260$, standing)~\cite  {vialle2005}. This cohort is supine, and the two postural measures depart in opposite directions --- sacral slope 4.1\si {\degree } lower, pelvic tilt 4.3\si {\degree } higher. That is one discrepancy, not two: $PI = PT + SS$ holds by construction, so with pelvic incidence at its published value any fall in one appears as an equal rise in the other.}}{13}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:spinopelvic}{{II}{13}{Derived measures against a published asymptomatic reference ($n=260$, standing)~\cite {vialle2005}. This cohort is supine, and the two postural measures depart in opposite directions --- sacral slope 4.1\si {\degree } lower, pelvic tilt 4.3\si {\degree } higher. That is one discrepancy, not two: $PI = PT + SS$ holds by construction, so with pelvic incidence at its published value any fall in one appears as an equal rise in the other}{table.2}{}}
